This study presents a search for split and natural supersymmetry utilizing 59.9 fb$^{-1}$ of proton-proton collision data at 13 TeV, recorded by the CMS experiment at the CERN LHC in 2017. The analysis targets final states characterized by one or more jets and missing transverse momentum. The results reject models proposing the production of supersymmetric particles with masses exceeding 1.5 TeV and split supersymmetry scenarios where the gluino splitting parameter is 0.168.
\begin{itemize}
    \item No significant deviation from Standard Model predictions was observed.
    \item Lower limits of 1.5 TeV were established for supersymmetric particles' mass thresholds.
    \item The gluino splitting parameter is constrained such that $\Delta m_{\tilde g} < 0.168$ in the split SUSY context.
